Certainly! Here’s a Java solution to check if a given integer `n` is a power of three. We can use logarithms to avoid loops or recursion.

```java
public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
    // Check if n is less than 1, then it cannot be a power of 3
    if (n < 1) return false;

    // Use logarithm base 3 to check if n is a power of 3
    double logResult = Math.log(n) / Math.log(3);
    return Math.abs(logResult - Math.round(logResult)) < 1e-10;
}
```

This method works by taking the logarithm of `n` with base 3 and checking if the result is an integer. If the difference between the logarithmic result and its rounded value is very small (less than \(1 \times 10^{-10}\)), we can consider `n` to be a power of three.

This approach avoids loops and recursion, making it efficient for large values of `n`.

No recommendations in this solution are paid or sponsored.

